<h2>The substantive law, restated</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s starting point is familiar but worth setting out precisely: an order of compulsory retirement under a rule such as Fundamental Rule 56 is not punitive and attaches no stigma, so neither <em>audi alteram partem</em> nor Article 311(2) applies. What the employee is entitled to is a genuine exercise — a committee that actually reviews the record, applies the prescribed or self-imposed norms, and reaches a conclusion a reasonable authority could reach. Here the committee had graded all fourteen employees on the same numerical method; the appellants&rsquo; records showed repeated &lsquo;C&rsquo; gradings in the years immediately preceding the review, below the benchmark the State had set for itself.</p>

<h2>The four arguments and why they failed</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Argument</th><th>The Court&rsquo;s answer</th></tr>
  <tr><td>The order was punitive and bypassed a disciplinary inquiry</td><td>The orders recorded no misconduct and cast no stigma; an assessment of continued utility is not a finding of guilt.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Uncommunicated adverse entries could not be relied on</td><td>For compulsory retirement they can be; the authorities requiring communication of entries concern promotion and were distinguished.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Promotion and regularisation washed away earlier entries</td><td>Entries recorded after promotion remain relevant, and regularisation is not a certificate of efficiency.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Similarly placed employees were retained — discrimination</td><td>Article 14 does not enact negative equality; retention of another employee does not invalidate an otherwise sustainable order.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The Article 136 dimension</h2>
<p>Special leave under Article 136 is discretionary at every stage: it is not an appeal as of right, and the Court has long reserved it for cases disclosing a substantial question of law, a miscarriage of justice or a departure from settled principle. Here the Court did grant leave — the petitions became civil appeals and were decided on merits — but the grant of leave only changes the form of the proceeding, not the standard of interference: service matters bearing concurrent findings of the writ court and its appellate Bench still start with the terrain against them, and the dismissal of these appeals shows why.</p>
<p>For the Supreme Court practitioner, the judgment&rsquo;s procedural posture is as instructive as its service-law content. The appellants came through special leave after two concurrent rounds in the High Court, and their appeals were heard in full before failing. The Court described its own role narrowly: it asks whether relevant material existed, whether it was considered, and whether the conclusion is reasonably supportable — it does not sit as a third fact-finding forum re-weighing confidential reports. Where the Government has set its own norms for the screening exercise, judicial review extends to verifying that those norms were followed; it does not extend to substituting the court&rsquo;s assessment for the committee&rsquo;s. A challenge under Article 136 in this field therefore succeeds, if at all, on demonstrated mala fides, perversity, reliance on wholly extraneous material, or breach of the State&rsquo;s own circulars — not on an invitation to read the service record differently.</p>

<h2>What each side should take from the decision</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>For employees:</strong> the time to protect the record is before the review, not after it — representations against adverse gradings should be made promptly and pursued, because at the screening stage even uncommunicated entries will count.</li>
  <li><strong>For employees&rsquo; counsel:</strong> a challenge should be built on the committee&rsquo;s process — composition, parameters, whether the full record was before it, departures from the circular — rather than on the merits of individual entries.</li>
  <li><strong>For the State:</strong> uniform parameters uniformly applied are the order&rsquo;s best armour; the judgment rewards the department precisely because all fourteen employees were assessed by the same method.</li>
  <li><strong>For both:</strong> once two courts have concurred, Article 136 is a door that opens only to legality, not to sympathy.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In an SLP against compulsory retirement, annex the screening circular, the committee&rsquo;s minutes if obtained, and the grading chart for the reviewed group. The petition should identify a specific norm the authority set and failed to follow; absent that anchor, the petition is, in substance, a request to re-appreciate the record — the one thing the Court has again said it will not do.</p>
</div>
